## 1. Objective and scope

Develop and test a state-space explanation for persistent tensile-strength differences among nominally identical 3D-printed polymer coupons.

The study will:

- Estimate how latent manufacturing states evolve during printing, storage, conditioning, and testing.
- Determine which measurable process, material, geometry, and test-system variables explain coupon-to-coupon, build-to-build, and machine-to-machine strength differences.
- Separate true specimen strength variation from measurement variation.
- Distinguish competing explanations, including:
  1. feedstock condition and composition;
  2. thermal history and interlayer bonding;
  3. extrusion-flow and geometric variation;
  4. machine-position or environmental effects;
  5. post-print aging or moisture uptake;
  6. test-machine, gripping, alignment, and strain-rate effects;
  7. stable machine/build offsets not captured by nominal settings.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength. Secondary outcomes: elastic modulus, yield stress, strain at break, toughness, fracture location, and fracture mode.

The initial scope is one polymer formulation, one coupon standard and orientation, one nominal process recipe, and a prespecified set of printers, material lots, builds, and test systems. Results will apply only to the studied material-process-test domain unless replicated elsewhere.

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means identical digital geometry and nominal settings, not necessarily identical realized material or process histories.
- Each coupon has an evolving latent state \(x_t\), only partially observed through sensors and later measurements. Candidate state components include:
  - effective material moisture and degradation;
  - melt/road temperature;
  - extrusion-flow condition;
  - local cooling and chamber condition;
  - interlayer-contact quality and residual stress;
  - post-print environmental condition;
  - effective load-train alignment and grip condition during testing.
- Observed process data \(z_t\) are noisy measurements of this state:
  \[
  x_{t+1}=f(x_t,u_t,c)+w_t,\qquad z_t=h(x_t)+v_t
  \]
  where \(u_t\) contains controlled inputs, \(c\) contains specimen/build context, and \(w_t,v_t\) are process and measurement errors.
- Tensile response is an observation of the terminal specimen and test-system states:
  \[
  y_i=g(x^{\text{print}}_i,x^{\text{storage}}_i,x^{\text{test}}_i,q_i)+\epsilon_i
  \]
  where \(q_i\) contains measured geometry and defects.
- Coupon outcomes may be correlated within material spool, build, printer, position, conditioning batch, operator, tester, and time period.
- Strength differences could reflect mean shifts, increased state variability, or both.
- Instrument calibration, sensor latency, clock synchronization, missingness, censoring, and destructive-test variability must be modeled rather than assumed negligible.
- Causal claims will be limited to randomized or deliberately manipulated factors. Associations involving passively observed states will be described as predictive unless supported by intervention.

## 3. Research design

### A. Phased design

**Phase 0: measurement-system qualification**

Before studying manufacturing causes:

- Calibrate load cells, extensometers, dimensional instruments, balances, temperature sensors, humidity sensors, and extrusion-flow proxies.
- Conduct tensile-system repeatability and reproducibility studies using stable reference specimens or an appropriate transfer artifact.
- Quantify operator, grip, alignment, extensometer, and tester contributions.
- Verify common timestamps across printer, environmental, imaging, and tensile systems.
- Establish repeatable preprocessing rules before examining strength associations.

**Phase 1: baseline replicated production**

Produce a balanced baseline dataset under one nominal recipe. Randomize or block:

- printer;
- material lot and spool;
- build date and order;
- coupon position and orientation on the build plate;
- conditioning batch;
- test order;
- operator and tensile machine.

Include multiple coupons per build and repeated builds over time. Rotate spool-printer assignments where technically possible so spool, printer, and calendar time are not confounded. Randomize coupon locations within builds and preserve complete specimen genealogy.

This phase estimates variance components, detects persistent latent-state patterns, and identifies candidate state variables.

**Phase 2: randomized mechanism challenges**

Conduct targeted factorial or fractional-factorial interventions chosen from Phase 1 findings. Candidate manipulations include:

- material moisture: dried versus controlled humid exposure;
- nozzle-temperature trajectory;
- chamber/ambient temperature or cooling condition;
- print speed and flow multiplier;
- layer time or cooling interval;
- spool or resin lot;
- build-plate position;
- hold time and storage humidity before testing;
- tensile strain rate;
- grip method, pressure, alignment, or tester.

Use independent randomization where feasible and avoid changing several mechanistically linked factors together unless the design estimates their interaction. Include nominal-condition controls in every block.

**Phase 3: confirmatory replication and transfer**

Freeze the state-space model and decision criteria, then repeat the most discriminating interventions:

- on new material lots;
- on different days;
- on at least one additional printer or tensile system;
- with randomized test order and blinded specimen identifiers.

### B. Experimental unit and hierarchy

Define units explicitly:

- spool or material batch for material-treatment factors;
- build for whole-build environmental or machine factors;
- coupon for position and testing factors;
- time segment or deposited layer for dynamic process states.

Use split-plot analysis when randomization occurs at different levels. Do not treat time samples or layers from one coupon as independent coupon replicates.

### C. Sampling and sample size

Base sample size on the smallest tensile-strength difference considered practically important, not merely detectable. Use pilot variance components to simulate power under the hierarchical state-space model.

The design should provide:

- enough independent builds to estimate build-level variation;
- enough coupons per build to estimate within-build spatial variation;
- repeated printer-spool combinations to separate printer and material effects;
- enough intervention blocks to estimate interactions and temporal drift;
- reserved confirmatory data not used for model selection.

Specify expected attrition and invalid-test rules in advance. Increase independent builds rather than only increasing coupons within a build when build-level variation dominates.

### D. Competing explanations and discriminating observations

| Explanation | Expected observations | Observation inconsistent with explanation |
|---|---|---|
| Material moisture or degradation | Strength tracks measured moisture, drying history, spool exposure time, or spectroscopy/thermal indicators; randomized drying shifts state estimates and strength across printers | Persistent differences after controlled material conditioning, with no moisture-response gradient |
| Thermal history/interlayer bonding | Local strength tracks reconstructed melt/cooling histories, chamber conditions, and weld-quality imaging; nozzle/chamber interventions change both latent thermal state and fracture behavior | Thermal interventions alter logged temperature but not strength or fracture mode, while flow/geometry explains outcomes |
| Under/over-extrusion and void content | Strength tracks mass per volume, bead geometry, porosity, pressure or motor-load anomalies; flow intervention produces dose response | Differences remain after density, dimensions, porosity, and flow state are controlled |
| Spatial machine/environment effect | Plate-position gradients recur across builds and rotate or disappear when airflow, orientation, or machine condition is changed | No reproducible spatial pattern across randomized layouts |
| Post-print conditioning | Strength changes predictably with storage duration, humidity, or temperature; conditioning intervention shifts strength without changing printed geometry | Fresh and conditioned coupons are equivalent across adequately separated exposure levels |
| Test-system artifact | Results follow tensile machine, operator, grip setup, strain rate, or alignment; crossed retesting with reference artifacts reveals bias | Differences follow specimen genealogy after randomized, calibrated, blinded testing |
| Stable unmeasured printer/build state | Persistent printer/build innovations remain after observed covariates; state estimates forecast later coupons within the same run | Residuals are exchangeable and no temporal persistence remains |
| Digital geometry/orientation or slicing artifact | Differences follow slicer version, toolpath, seam, raster orientation, or file checksum | Effects persist with locked files, verified toolpaths, and randomized seam/orientation controls |

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Specimen genealogy

For every coupon, record:

- unique identifier;
- CAD and slicing-file checksums;
- software and firmware versions;
- all nominal settings;
- material manufacturer, formulation, color, lot, spool, and spool history;
- printer, nozzle, maintenance history, and accumulated use;
- build, position, orientation, print sequence, and timestamps;
- conditioning, transport, and storage history;
- tensile machine, fixture, operator, calibration state, and test order.

### Dynamic process evidence

Collect synchronized time-series or layer-level data where available:

- commanded and measured nozzle, bed, chamber, and ambient temperatures;
- ambient relative humidity;
- print speed, acceleration, layer time, cooling-fan command;
- filament feed, extrusion command, motor current or load, and any pressure proxy;
- energy consumption or heater duty cycle;
- interruptions, alarms, pauses, and maintenance events;
- thermal or visible imagery of deposition and cooling;
- local position and toolpath information aligned to sensor traces.

### Material and coupon evidence

- Feedstock diameter distribution, moisture content, and mass.
- Relevant composition, thermal, rheological, or chemical checks on sampled material.
- Coupon dimensions at multiple gauge locations.
- Coupon mass, apparent density, surface texture, warpage, and raster/seam placement.
- Internal void fraction and defect morphology from nondestructive imaging on a prespecified subset.
- Fractography and failure-location classification after testing.
- Witness coupons or process-control specimens in each build.

### Tensile evidence

Retain raw force, displacement, extensometer strain, and time signals—not only summary values. Record preload, strain rate, grip pressure, alignment checks, slippage indicators, fracture location, and invalid-test reasons.

## 5. Measurement

### A. Outcome definitions

Predefine algorithms for:

- ultimate tensile strength using measured cross-sectional area;
- modulus over a specified strain interval;
- yield stress and yield definition;
- strain at break;
- toughness;
- valid versus invalid fracture location;
- grip failure, slip, or sensor saturation.

Perform sensitivity analyses using nominal versus measured dimensions and alternative defensible area estimates.

### B. State measurements

Map each sensor to a candidate state, its sampling rate, calibration uncertainty, spatial support, and expected delay. Because nozzle thermistor readings may not equal polymer-road temperature, treat them as noisy proxies rather than direct truth.

Use synchronized timestamps and estimate sensor lag. Summarize dynamic histories only after retaining raw data. Candidate features include:

- peak and mean conditions;
- heating/cooling slopes;
- time above bonding-relevant thresholds;
- layer-to-layer variability;
- spatial gradients;
- transient deviations and change points;
- cumulative exposure or state dwell time.

### C. Test-system control

- Calibrate according to a prespecified schedule.
- Randomize and blind test order.
- Use consistent grip insertion depth and alignment procedures.
- Record grip pressure and apply alignment checks.
- Measure strain with an extensometer where feasible.
- Use reference specimens across machines and days.
- Conduct duplicate dimensional measurements and blinded reanalysis of a subset.
- Separate operator from machine effects through crossed assignments.

### D. Data quality

Define in advance:

- permissible sensor dropout and interpolation limits;
- timestamp tolerances;
- outlier flags;
- test invalidation criteria;
- rules for sensor replacement or recalibration;
- missing-data codes that distinguish technical failure from absent measurement.

Preserve excluded observations with reasons and analyze exclusions for dependence on treatment or latent state.

## 6. Analysis

### A. Primary state-space model

Fit a hierarchical multivariate state-space model. At a suitable time or layer resolution:

\[
x_{i,t}=A x_{i,t-1}+B u_{i,t}+G r_i+b_{\text{printer}}+b_{\text{build}}+w_{i,t}
\]

\[
z_{i,t}=H x_{i,t}+v_{i,t}
\]

\[
y_i=\alpha+\beta^\top x_{i,T}+\gamma^\top q_i+
b_{\text{lot}}+b_{\text{spool}}+b_{\text{printer}}+
b_{\text{build}}+b_{\text{tester}}+\epsilon_i
\]

Here, \(r_i\) contains randomized interventions, \(q_i\) contains measured realized geometry and defects, and random effects represent the sampling hierarchy. Introduce nonlinear transitions, distributed thermal-history effects, or interactions only when prespecified or supported by held-out predictive performance.

Estimate:

- latent-state trajectories and uncertainty;
- intervention effects on states and tensile outcomes;
- direct and state-mediated effects;
- variance contributions at material, printer, build, position, coupon, and tester levels;
- persistence, drift, and change points;
- remaining unexplained strength variation.

### B. Model comparison

Compare prespecified candidate models:

1. test-system-only;
2. material-state;
3. thermal/bonding-state;
4. flow/geometry/porosity-state;
5. conditioning-state;
6. combined model;
7. combined model with persistent unobserved printer/build state.

Use held-out builds or forward-chaining prediction rather than random row-wise splitting. Assess predictive log score or likelihood, calibration, residual variance, and forecasting of future coupons. Penalize unnecessary state dimensions and test identifiability through simulation using the planned design.

### C. Causal analysis

Estimate randomized treatment effects according to assignment, with per-protocol estimates secondary. For mechanistic mediation, evaluate whether an intervention:

1. shifts the intended measured or latent state;
2. shifts tensile strength;
3. reduces the strength effect after accounting for that state;
4. produces the predicted fracture, porosity, or geometry signature.

Do not declare mediation solely because a covariate reduces a regression coefficient; require temporal ordering, intervention relevance, and uncertainty propagation.

### D. Residual diagnostics and falsification

Examine whether residuals retain:

- temporal autocorrelation;
- printer, spool, build, plate-position, or tester clustering;
- heteroscedasticity;
- nonlinear dependence on predicted strength;
- associations with omitted process traces;
- fracture-mode-specific patterns.

Use negative controls where available, such as sensor channels with no plausible causal relation or build regions not affected by a localized intervention. Check whether nominal controls reproduce baseline state distributions.

### E. Sensitivity analyses

- Alternative latent-state dimensions and priors.
- Alternative strength and area calculations.
- Sensor lag and calibration uncertainty.
- Missing-not-at-random scenarios.
- Exclusion versus inclusion of grip failures.
- Robust error distributions.
- Models excluding post-outcome information.
- Separate analyses by fracture mode.
- Leave-one-printer, lot, build, and tester-out validation.
- Analysis with and without high-cost imaging variables to assess practical deployability.

### F. Interpretation

A factor will be considered explanatory only if it has:

- a reproducible association with the outcome;
- correct temporal ordering;
- a randomized or otherwise credible source of variation;
- a state trajectory and physical signature consistent with its proposed mechanism;
- replication on held-out builds;
- a meaningful reduction in unexplained variance or improved out-of-sample prediction.

Report effect estimates and uncertainty, not only significance tests.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Before data collection, define:

- smallest practically important strength difference;
- minimum useful reduction in unexplained variance;
- acceptable predictive error;
- maximum tolerable test-system contribution;
- evidence thresholds for mechanism confirmation and rejection.

A competing explanation is supported when:

1. its intervention changes the intended state;
2. the predicted tensile response occurs with a confidence or credible interval excluding the prespecified negligible range;
3. the expected ancillary signature—such as porosity, spatial gradient, or fracture mode—also occurs;
4. the result replicates in confirmatory data;
5. the model improves held-out prediction beyond the stated threshold.

It is rejected or deprioritized when the intervention achieves sufficient separation in the proposed state but produces neither a practically important strength change nor its predicted ancillary signature, with uncertainty narrow enough to exclude the target effect.

Stop adding baseline builds when variance-component and state-persistence estimates stabilize to prespecified precision. Stop targeted experimentation when:

- one or more mechanisms meet confirmatory criteria and residual variability is below the practical threshold; or
- all planned mechanism challenges have adequate power to exclude practically important effects; or
- additional runs fail to improve held-out prediction or reduce key parameter uncertainty by a prespecified amount.

Do not stop solely after obtaining statistical significance. Allow safety, equipment-failure, or material-unavailability stopping rules, with affected blocks repeated under the original randomization logic where possible.

## 8. Limitations

- Latent states may not be uniquely identifiable when several physical mechanisms produce similar sensor patterns.
- Common printer sensors may be inaccurate proxies for polymer-road temperature, pressure, or weld quality.
- Some diagnostic measurements, especially internal imaging and fractography, may be too costly for every coupon.
- Destructive tensile testing prevents repeated measurement of the same specimen under different test conditions.
- Hierarchical interventions may have low effective sample size even when many coupons are tested.
- Drying, thermal, flow, and conditioning manipulations may interact, limiting simple attribution.
- Test artifacts can be reduced but not completely eliminated.
- The selected coupon geometry may not represent complex parts, alternate raster orientations, or multiaxial loading.
- Findings may not generalize to other polymers, colors, additives, nozzle sizes, printer architectures, or environmental ranges.
- A model can predict strength well while misrepresenting the physical mechanism; intervention, ancillary signatures, and replication remain necessary.
- Leaving persistent residual states in the final model may improve forecasting without identifying their physical cause; such states should be labeled unresolved rather than assigned a speculative mechanism.

